## Install

1. Drop the `Scripts/` folder into your project's `Assets/`.
2. Add the `{{ s.name }}` component to a GameObject (e.g. an NPC or a DialogueManager).
3. Wire your UI to the events, then call `StartDialogue()`{% if variant == "barks" %} or `PlayBark()`{% endif %}:

```csharp
var dlg = GetComponent<{{ s.name }}>();
dlg.OnLine += (speaker, text) => { nameLabel.text = speaker; bodyLabel.text = text; };
{% if variant == "branching" %}dlg.OnChoices += labels => BuildChoiceButtons(labels);   // each button calls dlg.Choose(i)
{% endif %}dlg.OnDialogueEnd += () => panel.SetActive(false);
dlg.StartDialogue();
```

## How it advances

{% if variant == "branching" %}
Branching tree. Each node may offer choices; `OnChoices` gives you the labels, and you call
`Choose(index)` to follow that branch. A choice with an empty `next` ends the conversation.
{% elif variant == "cutscene" %}
Auto-advancing cutscene: lines play on a {{ "%.1f"|format(s.auto_advance_delay) }}s timer — no input needed.
{% elif variant == "barks" %}
Random one-off barks: call `PlayBark()` for an ambient line. No progression.
{% elif variant == "localized" %}
Linear sequence whose `text` values are **localization keys** — override `Resolve(string)` to map
them through your string table (e.g. Unity Localization). Call `Advance()` to continue.
{% else %}
Linear sequence. Call `Advance()` from your continue button / input to show the next line.
{% endif %}
{% if s.text_render == "typewriter" %}
Text reveals one character at a time at {{ "%.0f"|format(s.text_speed) }} chars/sec (`OnLine` fires repeatedly as it types).
{% endif %}

